<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter10_static-members-and-class-level-state">
    <title>Static Members and Class-Level State</title>
    <p>
        Most data members belong to individual objects. Each
        <c>StudentAccount</c>, for example, has its own balance. Sometimes a
        class also needs one value shared by every object of that class. A
        <term>static data member</term> belongs to the class as a whole rather
        than to one particular object.
    </p>

    <p>
        A static member is useful when the class must keep track of class-level
        information, such as the number of objects that have been created. It
        is not a replacement for ordinary object state: each account still has
        its own balance, while all accounts can share one account count.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_static-data-members">
        <title>Static Data Members Are Shared</title>

        <p>
            Declare a static data member inside the class, then provide its
            definition outside the class. The definition creates the one shared
            object that belongs to the class. In C++17, this traditional form is
            still clear and useful, so we will use it here.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_static_account_count">
            <title>One shared count and one balance per account</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_static_account_count"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class StudentAccount {
private:
    double balance;
    static int total_accounts_created;

public:
    StudentAccount()
        : balance{0.0} {
        ++total_accounts_created;
    }

    void deposit_money(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
    }

    double get_balance() const {
        return balance;
    }

    static int get_total_accounts_created() {
        return total_accounts_created;
    }
};

int StudentAccount::total_accounts_created = 0;

int main() {
    StudentAccount first_account;
    StudentAccount second_account;

    first_account.deposit_money(100.00);

    std::cout << first_account.get_balance() << '\n';
    std::cout << second_account.get_balance() << '\n';
    std::cout << StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            Each object has a separate <c>balance</c>, so depositing money into
            <c>first_account</c> does not change <c>second_account</c>. The
            static member <c>total_accounts_created</c> has only one shared
            value, so both constructor calls update the same count.
        </p>

        <p>
            The line
            <c>int StudentAccount::total_accounts_created = 0;</c>
            is the out-of-class definition. The scope-resolution operator
            <c>::</c> says that this definition belongs to
            <c>StudentAccount</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <em>Important:</em> This simple counter increases only when the
            default constructor runs. If the class later defines a copy
            constructor and the count is intended to include copied objects,
            that copy constructor must also increment the static counter. Copy
            assignment does not create a new object, so it should not increase
            an object-creation count.
        </p>
    </subsection>


    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_static-member-functions">
        <title>Static Member Functions</title>

        <p>
            A <term>static member function</term> also belongs to the class
            rather than to one object. It can be called with the class name and
            the scope-resolution operator:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_static_function_call"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int number_of_accounts = StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created();
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            A non-static member function receives an implicit object parameter,
            represented inside the function by <c>this</c>. A static member
            function has no implicit object parameter and therefore has no
            <c>this</c> pointer. It can access static members directly, but it
            must use an object or a reference parameter to work with a
            non-static member.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_instance-static-functions">
            <title>Instance and static member functions</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="24%"/>
                <col width="24%"/>
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="24%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Feature</cell>
                    <cell>Instance member function</cell>
                    <cell>Static member function</cell>
                    <cell>Typical use</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Belongs to</cell>
                    <cell>One object</cell>
                    <cell>The class</cell>
                    <cell>Choose according to the state needed.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Call</cell>
                    <cell><c>account.get_balance()</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created()</c></cell>
                    <cell>Use the class name for class-wide behavior.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Has <c>this</c>?</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>No</cell>
                    <cell>Static functions have no current object.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Directly accesses</cell>
                    <cell>Static and non-static members</cell>
                    <cell>Static members only</cell>
                    <cell>Pass an object if non-static state is needed.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>const</c> after the function</cell>
                    <cell>May be <c>const</c></cell>
                    <cell>Cannot be <c>const</c></cell>
                    <cell>There is no object for a static function to promise not to modify.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_static-member-design-guidance">
        <title>When Should a Member Be Static?</title>

        <p>
            Make a member static when the value or operation describes the class
            as a whole and does not depend on one object's state. A count of all
            accounts is class-level information. A particular account's balance
            is object-level information and should remain a non-static member.
        </p>

        <p>
            A static member does not automatically make a class safer. Because
            every object shares the same static state, changing that state can
            affect all objects. Keep the static data member private when outside
            code should not change it directly, and provide a carefully named
            public member function when access is needed.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_static_no-this"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
static int get_total_accounts_created() {
    return total_accounts_created;
    // return balance;  // Error: no particular account is available.
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The commented expression cannot work because a call such as
            <c>StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created()</c> does not select
            a particular account. The function can read the shared count, but
            it cannot guess which object's balance the caller means.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_static-codelens">
        <title>Tracing Shared Static State</title>

        <p>
            Step through the example to observe two separate balances and one
            shared object count.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter10_static_members"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Counter%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20int%20value%3B%0A%20%20%20%20static%20int%20created_count%3B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Counter%28%29%20%3A%20value%7B0%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%2B%2Bcreated_count%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%20%20%20%20static%20int%20get_created_count%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20return%20created_count%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20Counter%3A%3Acreated_count%20%3D%200%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Counter%20first%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Counter%20second%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20Counter%3A%3Aget_created_count%28%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_static-data-member-initialization">
        <title>Declaring and Initializing a Static Data Member</title>

        <p>
            There are two important ways to define a non-constant static data
            member. The traditional form declares the member inside the class
            and defines it once outside the class. The C++17 form uses
            <c>inline static</c> and defines the member directly inside the
            class.
        </p>

        <p>
            The traditional form is useful when the class declaration is in a
            header file and the definition is placed in one implementation
            file. The class declaration announces the member, while the
            out-of-class definition supplies its shared storage and initial
            value.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_traditional-static-definition">
            <title>Traditional static data member definition</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_traditional-static-definition"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class VisitCounter {
private:
    static int visit_count;

public:
    VisitCounter() {
        ++visit_count;
    }

    static int get_visit_count() {
        return visit_count;
    }
};

// The class declaration above does not allocate the shared member.
int VisitCounter::visit_count = 0;

int main() {
    VisitCounter first_visit;
    VisitCounter second_visit;

    std::cout << VisitCounter::get_visit_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            C++17 introduced <c>inline</c> variables. An
            <c>inline static</c> data member may be initialized inside the
            class definition:
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10-inline-static-definition">
            <title>C++17 in-class definition with <c>inline static</c></title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10-inline-static-definition"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class VisitCounter {
private:
    inline static int visit_count = 0;

public:
    VisitCounter() {
        ++visit_count;
    }

    static int get_visit_count() {
        return visit_count;
    }
};

int main() {
    VisitCounter first_visit;
    VisitCounter second_visit;

    std::cout << VisitCounter::get_visit_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The <c>inline</c> keyword here does not mean that a function should
            be expanded at its call site, and it does not promise a performance
            improvement. It allows the static data member to be defined in the
            class definition, including a header file, while still representing
            one shared member across the program. This is why
            <c>inline static</c> is especially convenient in C++17.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_static-data-member-forms">
            <title>Forms of static data member definition</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Form</cell>
                    <cell>Where it is defined</cell>
                    <cell>Typical use</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>static int count;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Declaration inside the class</cell>
                    <cell>Used with one separate out-of-class definition.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>int ClassName::count = 0;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Outside the class</cell>
                    <cell>Traditional form; define it once in an implementation file.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>inline static int count = 0;</c></cell>
                    <cell>Inside the class</cell>
                    <cell>C++17 form; convenient for a class defined in a header.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            These forms do not create different kinds of object state. In each
            case, all objects of the class share one static data member. They
            differ in where the definition and initialization are written.
        </p>

        <p>
            An integral constant has another common form. For example,
            <c>static constexpr int maximum_attempts = 3;</c> can be defined
            inside a class. In C++17, a static <c>constexpr</c> data member is
            implicitly inline. This form is appropriate for a named constant,
            not for a value that changes as objects are created.
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <exercise label="chapter10_static_exercise_shared-state">
        <statement>
            <p>Which statement best describes a static data member?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>All objects of the class share one value for that member.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A static data member belongs to the class as a whole.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Each object receives a separate copy of the member.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>That describes an ordinary non-static data member.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The member can be used only inside a constructor.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Static members can be used by appropriate member functions throughout the class.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The member must always be public.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A static data member can be private, protected, or public.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_static_exercise-no-this">
        <statement>
            <p>Why can a static member function not directly read an ordinary data member such as <c>balance</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>It has no <c>this</c> pointer and therefore no particular object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A static function can access shared static state, but no individual object's state without an object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Static functions cannot read any data.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>They can directly read static data members.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The member must first be made public.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Access control is not the reason; the missing object is.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A static function always runs before the class is defined.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Static describes class membership, not source-code order.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_static_parsons">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the lines to create two objects and then read the shared count.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>StudentAccount first_account;</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>StudentAccount second_account;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>int count = StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created();</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_static_exercise-call-syntax">
        <statement>
            <p>Which call most clearly communicates that the function uses class-level state?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>StudentAccount::get_total_accounts_created()</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The class name and scope-resolution operator identify a static member call.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>get_total_accounts_created(StudentAccount)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The class name is not passed as a function argument.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>StudentAccount.get_total_accounts_created()</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>C++ uses <c>::</c> with a class name; the dot operator is used with an object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>static.StudentAccount.get_total_accounts_created()</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p><c>static</c> is part of the declaration, not a call prefix.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_static_exercise-definition">
        <statement>
            <p>Why is an out-of-class definition needed for the traditional static data member declaration?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>The class declaration announces the member, and the definition provides the shared storage.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The out-of-class definition creates the one class-level object used by the program.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It creates a separate static member for every object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The purpose is one shared member, not one per object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It changes the member into a local variable.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The scope-resolution operator keeps the definition associated with the class.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It is required only for static member functions.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This traditional definition concerns static data members.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_static_exercise-inline-definition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which C++17 declaration defines and initializes a shared static
                data member directly inside the class?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>inline static int count = 0;</c></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        In C++17, <c>inline static</c> permits the definition
                        and initialization inside the class.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>static int count;</c></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        This is only the traditional in-class declaration; it
                        still needs an out-of-class definition.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>int static::count = 0;</c></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The scope-resolution operator follows the class name,
                        as in <c>ClassName::count</c>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>static inline count;</c></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        The declaration needs a type and an initializer, such
                        as <c>inline static int count = 0;</c>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
